# Rank 1014 / CVE-2021-39197: better_errors exposes an in-browser REPL in
# development, and its internal endpoints accepted cross-site requests. A
# malicious page could POST to the local endpoint and reach
# @error_page.send("do_#{opts[:method]}", ...), executing Ruby in the developer's
# application (CWE-352 leading to code execution). The fixed revision issues a
# BetterErrors-CSRF-Token cookie with the error page and requires the request
# body to echo it before dispatching.
#
# The pool row for this rank named apache/arrow CVE-2024-52338 as ruby, but that
# advisory states the flaw affects only the arrow R package, and VyQL has no R
# frontend, so scanning it as ruby cannot succeed. The row names the next
# unranked ruby candidate, keeping the rank number stable.
#
# ATTENTION, on two counts, both measured.
#  - The mitigation is a hand-rolled double-submit cookie comparison
#    (request.cookies[NAME] == body['csrfToken']), not a named framework call.
#    Every core.CsrfProtection binding recognises a named API instead
#    (protect_from_forgery, verify_authenticity_token, validate_csrf_token,
#    verifyCsrfToken, check_admin_referer). A CSRF rule therefore cannot see
#    this control, so it would report the patched code exactly as it reports the
#    vulnerable code.
#  - The reflective dispatch itself is unmodelled: ruby binds only require and
#    load as dynamic-load sinks, and code.ReflectionInvoke (CWE-470,
#    VYQL-DESER-003) has Java bindings only, though ruby send, public_send and
#    __send__ are its direct equivalents. Adding them is measured and rejected:
#    on activeadmin, an ordinary Ruby project of 382 files, a
#    send binding produces 29 high-severity findings and the narrower
#    public_send/__send__ binding 9, with no true positive in either.
#    All are framework metaprogramming whose method name comes from
#    configuration rather than a request (send(symbol_or_proc, *args),
#    router.send(verb, *args), chain.public_send scope.scope_method). The cause
#    is that library-mode param-source labels public method parameters as
#    external input, so every reflective call inside a library becomes tainted.
#    The finding would also not have discriminated this fix, which keeps the
#    send call and only adds the token check.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-cost

test "rank1014: internal REPL endpoint dispatches request input with no CSRF check"
  lang ruby
  file lib/better_errors/middleware.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-003
  code
  ```
  module BetterErrors
    class Middleware
      def internal_call(env, opts)
        return no_errors_json_response unless @error_page
        return invalid_error_json_response if opts[:id] != @error_page.id

        env["rack.input"].rewind
        response = @error_page.send("do_#{opts[:method]}", JSON.parse(env["rack.input"].read))
        [200, { "Content-Type" => "text/plain; charset=utf-8" }, [JSON.dump(response)]]
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1014: fix requires the request to echo the CSRF cookie before dispatching"
  lang ruby
  file lib/better_errors/middleware.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-003
  code
  ```
  module BetterErrors
    class Middleware
      CSRF_TOKEN_COOKIE_NAME = 'BetterErrors-CSRF-Token'

      def internal_call(env, opts)
        return no_errors_json_response unless @error_page
        return invalid_error_json_response if opts[:id] != @error_page.id

        request = Rack::Request.new(env)
        return invalid_csrf_token_json_response unless request.cookies[CSRF_TOKEN_COOKIE_NAME]

        request.body.rewind
        body = JSON.parse(request.body.read)
        return invalid_csrf_token_json_response unless request.cookies[CSRF_TOKEN_COOKIE_NAME] == body['csrfToken']

        response = @error_page.send("do_#{opts[:method]}", body)
        [200, { "Content-Type" => "application/json; charset=utf-8" }, [JSON.dump(response)]]
      end
    end
  end
  ```
